
<p>Juli Clover, MacRumors:</p>

<blockquote>
  <p>It’s likely the iPhone Duo doesn’t support the Apple Pencil Pro
because there’s no magnetic surface for charging the pencil. The
Apple Pencil Pro charges magnetically when connected to a
compatible iPad, while the Apple Pencil with USB-C charges with a
USB-C port hidden behind a sliding cap.</p>
</blockquote>

<p>Charging is the least of the problems. Because the Duo doesn’t have the magnetic attachment for Pencil Pro, there’s no way to even <em>pair</em> a Pencil Pro. The easiest way to think about it isn’t to compare which devices support which features (like pressure sensitivity, or double-tapping) — it’s just to think about which devices can even <em>pair</em> with which Pencil models. Unless I’m missing an exception in <a href="https://support.apple.com/en-us/108937">Apple’s compatibility support document</a>, if a Pencil-compatible iPad or iPhone can pair with a certain Pencil’s connector, then it can use that Pencil. The iPads with both the magnetic attachment point and USB-C can use either the Pencil Pro or regular Pencil, and the devices without the magnetic attachment can only use the regular Pencil.</p>

<p>But the bottom line is that Apple’s entire Pencil compatibility story remains a mess. Imagine you own an iPad Pro and a Pencil Pro, and you love using the Pencil. You’ve always wanted to be able to use a Pencil with your iPhone. Now you can, if you buy a Duo. But you also have to buy a different Pencil — and then pack two different Pencils whenever you leave the house with both your iPad and iPhone. That’s ridiculous.</p>

